        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Glazed ceramic and porcelain are already sealed — vitrified in a kiln at temperatures no coating will improve on. Sealing them achieves nothing except a film that wears off unevenly. The tiles that genuinely need a sealer are the unglazed and natural ones, and they need quite different products.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="do-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Does Your Tile Actually Need Sealing?
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the question that should come first and almost never does. A great many tiled floors need no sealer on the tile at all, and buying one is money spent creating a problem rather than solving one.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Glazed ceramic and glazed porcelain do not need sealing.</strong> The glaze is a layer of glass fused to the tile body at well over a thousand degrees. It is already impervious — nothing you can buy in a bottle will improve on it, and nothing will penetrate it either. A sealer applied to glazed tile has nowhere to go, so it dries as a film on the surface. That film then wears off in traffic lanes and stays put under furniture, producing exactly the patchy appearance you were trying to avoid.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Unglazed tile does need sealing.</strong> Quarry tile, terracotta, saltillo, unglazed quarry and through-body porcelain all have an open pore structure at the surface. Water, oil and dirt go into those pores and stay there.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Natural stone almost always needs sealing.</strong> Slate, travertine, limestone, marble and honed granite are porous to varying degrees, and some of them are chemically vulnerable as well as physically absorbent.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Cement grout nearly always needs sealing</strong>, whatever the tile is. This is the part people skip and it is the part that actually discolours.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Water Test</h3>

        <p>
          Five minutes, no cost and no special equipment, and it settles the question definitively for any floor. Clean a small area and let it dry completely. Put several drops of water on the tile and several on a grout joint. Wait five minutes, then wipe.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the water still sits in beads and the surface underneath is unchanged, that material is not absorbing anything and does not need a sealer. If the water has flattened out and left a dark patch that slowly fades as it dries, it is being absorbed — and anything else spilled there will be absorbed too. Test the tile and the grout separately, because the answer is very frequently different for each of them.
        </p>

        <p>
          Run the same test annually on a sealed floor. It is a far better guide to when resealing is due than any interval printed on a bottle.
        </p>

        <h2 id="types" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Penetrating vs Topical
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every tile sealer is one of two things, and for floors the choice is nearly always straightforward.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Penetrating sealers</strong> — also called impregnators — carry active silanes or siloxanes into the pore structure of the tile, where they bond and line the pores. Nothing remains on the surface. The tile looks exactly as it did, keeps its own texture and slip resistance, and because there is no film there is nothing to peel, scuff or wear through unevenly. When the sealer eventually stops working it simply stops repelling; it does not fail visibly.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Topical sealers</strong> form a film on the surface. They can add sheen, they can be genuinely useful on saltillo and terracotta where a sacrificial coating is traditional, and they offer strong protection while they last. But they wear in traffic lanes, they can trap moisture underneath, they become slippery when wet, and eventually they need stripping rather than simply reapplying.
        </p>

        <p>
          For any floor that gets walked on and occasionally wet, use a penetrating sealer. Reserve topical products for decorative tile in low traffic, or for the specific case of terracotta where a wax or acrylic coating is part of the intended look. Our guide to <a href="/best-grout-sealer/">grout sealers</a> covers the same split as it applies to joints.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Enhancing Sealers</h3>

        <p>
          A third category worth knowing: enhancers are penetrating sealers that deliberately deepen the colour of the stone, producing the saturated look wet slate has in the rain. They protect exactly as an ordinary impregnator does. The only caution is that the effect is permanent and strong, so test on an offcut before committing a whole floor to it.
        </p>


