                  </div>

                  <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-gray-100">
                    <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-1">The Bottom Line</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2 id="by-tile" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Tile Type by Tile Type
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Glazed Ceramic and Porcelain</h3>
        <p>
          Seal the grout, leave the tile alone. This covers the majority of bathroom and kitchen floors installed in the last thirty years. Use a narrow applicator bottle to run sealer along the joints rather than flooding the whole floor.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Unglazed and Through-Body Porcelain</h3>
        <p>
          Genuinely porous despite the porcelain name, and worth sealing with a penetrating impregnator. Through-body porcelain is dense, so use a solvent-carried product that can get into what pore structure there is, and expect coverage per bottle to be high.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slate</h3>
        <p>
          Always seal. Slate is layered and absorbent, and it stains readily from oil in particular. This is the tile where an enhancing sealer most often earns its place, because the wet look is what most people wanted from slate in the first place. Expect it to drink the first coat and want a second.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Travertine and Limestone</h3>
        <p>
          Both are calcite stones: porous and also acid-sensitive, so lemon juice and vinegar will etch them regardless of sealing. A penetrating sealer stops staining but does not stop etching, and it is worth understanding that distinction before you are disappointed by it. Filled travertine also needs the fillers checked, since they pop out over time and leave open holes.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Saltillo and Terracotta</h3>
        <p>
          Extremely porous and traditionally finished with a topical coating rather than an impregnator. This is the one common case where a film-forming sealer is the right answer, because the look depends on it. Expect to strip and recoat every few years as part of ordinary maintenance.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Quarry Tile</h3>
        <p>
          Unglazed, absorbent and usually installed where it will get dirty — kitchens, entries, commercial spaces. A penetrating sealer with genuine oil repellency is the right choice, and it is worth paying for the better product here because the failure mode is greasy shadows that never come out.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shower Walls and Wet Rooms</h3>
        <p>
          Vertical tile in a shower is a different problem from a floor. The tile is usually glazed and needs nothing, but the grout is exposed to constant water and soap residue, which is the combination that grows the black staining people blame on the tile. Seal the joints with a penetrating sealer and expect to redo it more often than on a floor — every two years is a realistic interval in a shower that sees daily use by a whole household.
        </p>

        <p>
          Avoid film-forming products entirely on a shower floor. A topical coating there is both a genuine slip hazard and a trap for the moisture that inevitably works behind it, and stripping one out of a textured mosaic floor is a job nobody enjoys twice.
        </p>

        <h2 id="grout" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. The Grout Is the Real Job
        </h2>

        <p>
          On the majority of tiled floors the tile is impervious and the grout is not, which means every discoloured floor people want to fix is really a grout problem wearing a tile costume.
        </p>

        <p>
          Cement-based grout — sanded or unsanded — is porous by nature. It absorbs water, and with the water goes whatever the water is carrying: coffee, cooking oil, soil from shoes, soap residue. That is why grout joints go from pale grey to dark grey over a few years while the tiles either side stay pristine.
        </p>

        <p>
          A penetrating sealer in the joints prevents most of this, and it is genuinely worth the hour it takes. The important condition is timing: new cement grout must cure fully before sealing, which normally means at least 72 hours and longer in cool or humid conditions. Sealing green grout traps moisture in the joint and produces efflorescence — a chalky white bloom — that is far more annoying than the staining you were preventing.
        </p>

        <p>
          One exception worth knowing: <strong>epoxy grout does not need sealing.</strong> It is non-porous by formulation. If your floor was installed with epoxy grout, both the tile and the joints may be fully impervious and there is nothing here for you to do.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. How to Apply It
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean thoroughly and let it dry.</strong> A sealer locks in whatever is on the surface when it goes down, so any existing stain or residue becomes permanent. Use a pH-neutral cleaner rather than anything acidic, and allow a full 24 hours of drying — longer for stone, which holds moisture deeper than it looks.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Test in an inconspicuous spot.</strong> A closet, under an appliance, or an offcut. You are checking for colour change and for how quickly the material absorbs, which tells you how much product the floor will take.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Apply evenly and let it dwell.</strong> Use a brush, roller or applicator pad and work in manageable sections. The sealer needs time in contact with the surface to penetrate — usually five to fifteen minutes depending on the product. Keep the surface wet with sealer for that whole period; if a patch soaks in and goes dry, add more.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Buff off every trace of excess.</strong> This is the step that determines whether the job looks professional. Once the dwell time is up, wipe the surface thoroughly with clean dry cloths, turning them frequently. Any sealer left to dry on top will haze and streak, and removing it means solvent and effort.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Apply a second coat if the first was absorbed quickly.</strong> Porous tile such as slate and saltillo frequently drinks the first application. Wait the recommended interval and repeat until the surface stops taking it in.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Keep the floor dry while it cures.</strong> Light foot traffic after four to six hours is generally fine, but water should stay off for the full cure period — normally 24 to 72 hours. Mopping a floor on day one undoes a good deal of the work.
        </p>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing glazed porcelain or ceramic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The glaze is fired glass and is already impervious. A sealer cannot penetrate it, so it sits on top as a film, wears unevenly in traffic lanes and leaves you stripping a coating you never needed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using a topical sealer on a wet floor area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A film-forming sealer on a shower floor or an entry is a slip hazard when wet and traps any moisture that gets underneath it. Penetrating sealers are the correct choice anywhere water is routine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing before the grout has cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Cement grout needs a full cure — commonly around 72 hours, longer in cool or humid conditions — before anything seals it. Seal it early and you trap moisture in the joint, which causes efflorescence and a patchy finish.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping the test patch on stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Some sealers noticeably darken slate, travertine and saltillo. That may be exactly what you want, or it may ruin the look of the floor. Either way, find out on an offcut or in a closet, not in the middle of the room.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Leaving excess sealer to dry on the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A penetrating sealer that is not buffed off within its stated window leaves a hazy, streaky residue that is genuinely difficult to remove. Set a timer and wipe the surface properly.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do porcelain tiles need sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Glazed porcelain does not — the glaze is fired glass and nothing will penetrate it. Unglazed and through-body porcelain has a slightly porous surface and benefits from a penetrating sealer. Either way, cement grout between the tiles does need sealing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my tile needs sealing?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Put a few drops of water on a clean, dry tile and wait five minutes. If the water beads and the tile looks unchanged when you wipe it, it does not need sealing. If the water soaks in and leaves a dark patch that fades as it dries, it does.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sealer for ceramic tile?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">For glazed ceramic, none — seal the grout instead. For unglazed ceramic or quarry tile, a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer, which soaks into the pores and repels water and oil without changing the appearance or adding a slippery film.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should tile be resealed?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A quality penetrating sealer on a floor typically lasts three to five years, and up to ten on a wall or a low-traffic area. Rather than working to a schedule, re-run the water test annually — when water stops beading and starts darkening the surface, it is time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does tile sealer make tile slippery?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A topical, film-forming sealer can, particularly when wet, and that is the main argument against using one on floors. Penetrating sealers sit below the surface and leave the tile's own texture and slip resistance unchanged.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal tile and grout at the same time?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, if the tile itself needs sealing. Apply a penetrating sealer across the whole surface, let it soak into both tile and grout, then buff off the excess. On glazed tile, use a narrow applicator to put sealer only in the joints.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sealer for slate tile?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A penetrating sealer if you want to keep slate's natural matte appearance, or an enhancing sealer if you want the wet, saturated look that brings out the colour. Slate is genuinely porous and staining is a real risk, so this is one tile that always needs sealing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does tile sealer take to dry?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Most penetrating sealers are dry to the touch within an hour and accept light foot traffic after four to six. Full cure and water resistance typically take 24 to 72 hours, and the floor should stay dry for that whole period.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Related Sealing Guides
        </h2>

        <ul>
          <li><a href="/best-grout-sealer/">Best Grout Sealers</a> — for the joints, which almost always do need sealing.</li>
          <li><a href="/best-natural-stone-sealer/">Best Natural Stone Sealer</a> — for slate, travertine and limestone floors.</li>
          <li><a href="/best-marble-sealer/">Best Marble Sealer</a> — the specific case of polished calcite stone.</li>
          <li><a href="/best-granite-sealer/">Best Granite Sealer</a> — and how to run the water test that tells you if you need one.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            For most tiled floors in most houses, the tile itself needs nothing and the grout needs a penetrating sealer. Run the water test before you buy anything — it takes five minutes and it will save a great many people the price of a bottle.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            Where the tile is genuinely porous — slate, saltillo, quarry, unglazed porcelain — use a penetrating silane or siloxane rather than a film. It cannot peel, it cannot go slippery, and it does not change how the floor looks unless you have deliberately chosen an enhancer.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
